    "slug": "gartner-magic-quadrant-2026-bi",
    "title": "How to Evaluate a BI Platform Beyond Analyst Rankings",
    "excerpt": "Build a requirements matrix and a repeatable proof of concept without inventing analyst positions or scores.",
    "content": "<aside>Worked example with fictional data. AI-assisted editorial revision; no client performance results are claimed.</aside><h2 id=\"section-1\">Understand what is and is not being claimed</h2><p>This guide does not reproduce a Gartner chart or assert which vendors occupy particular positions in a 2026 report. Analyst research has a defined scope, publication date and usage terms. Read the actual licensed research before attributing a position or quotation to its publisher. A platform that appears attractive in an industry overview can still be unsuitable for a specific integration or operating model.</p><h2 id=\"section-2\">Turn preferences into requirements</h2><p>Imagine a fictional distributor comparing three BI platforms. Its non-negotiable needs are row-level access restrictions, a connection to its existing warehouse and a workable support process. Separate these gates from scored preferences such as visual styling. A product that fails a mandatory access test should not win because it receives more points for attractive charts. Record who can approve an exception to a gate.</p><h2 id=\"section-3\">Weight a small set of criteria</h2><p>Use weights that total 100 and publish a scoring rubric before running demonstrations. For example, give data integration 30, governance 30, operating effort 25 and authoring experience 15. With scores of 4, 3, 2 and 5 on a five-point scale, the weighted score is 3.35 out of 5. Those fictional inputs are a decision exercise, not vendor ratings. A second set of weights may reveal that the preferred platform depends strongly on one department's assumptions.</p><pre><code>Weighted score = (30*4 + 30*3 + 25*2 + 15*5) / 100\n               = 3.35 out of 5</code></pre><h2 id=\"section-4\">Run the same proof of concept</h2><p>Give each candidate the same sanitized data, question set, refresh requirement and access scenarios. Have a normal report author build the task, rather than evaluating only a specialist's polished demonstration. Include a changed source column, a failed refresh and a departing employee. Measure the work needed to recover. Keep configuration and license assumptions beside the results so a comparison does not quietly give one platform extra resources.</p><h2 id=\"section-5\">Write an explainable decision</h2><p>Summarize the mandatory tests, observed limitations, cost assumptions and exit plan. Describe why a rejected option failed your requirements instead of calling it universally inferior. Revisit the decision when workloads or product capabilities materially change. Analyst research can inform questions, but evidence from your own constrained trial should support the final selection.</p><h2 id=\"references\">Primary references</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.gartner.com/en/research/methodologies/magic-quadrants-research\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Gartner Magic Quadrant methodology</a></li></ul>",
