    "slug": "mastering-dax-patterns-2026",
    "title": "DAX Measures: Filter Context, Safe Ratios and Useful Variables",
    "excerpt": "Use a small revenue-and-cost example to explain measures and totals without promising universal performance gains.",
    "content": "<aside>Worked example with fictional data. AI-assisted editorial revision; no client performance results are claimed.</aside><h2 id=\"section-1\">Write the business meaning first</h2><p>Take a fictional Sales table with three lines: revenue and cost are (100, 60), (50, 45) and (150, 90). Total revenue is 300 and total cost is 195, so gross profit is 105. Gross margin for the complete set is 105/300 = 35%. Averaging the three row margins would answer a different question because the rows do not contribute equal revenue.</p><h2 id=\"section-2\">Define reusable measures</h2><p>Create simple base measures and use them in the ratio. DIVIDE lets the measure specify the result when the denominator is zero or blank; choose that result deliberately. A blank can mean that the ratio is not defined for the current selection. Replacing every blank with zero can imply that a business generated revenue at zero margin when it actually had no revenue.</p><pre><code>Revenue = SUM(Sales[Revenue])\nCost = SUM(Sales[Cost])\nGross Profit = [Revenue] - [Cost]\nGross Margin = DIVIDE([Gross Profit], [Revenue])</code></pre><h2 id=\"section-3\">Check filter context explicitly</h2><p>Put Region on a visual and compare its rows with the grand total. Each cell evaluates the measure in its own filter context; the grand total is not necessarily the sum or average of the displayed cells. If a region slicer does not affect the result, inspect relationships and any filter-removal expressions. Do not immediately add bidirectional filtering to force a visual to move; understand which table should filter which facts.</p><h2 id=\"section-4\">Use variables for readability</h2><p>A variable can give a meaningful name to an intermediate result. It is not a mutable spreadsheet cell. Consider where the variable is evaluated relative to a later context change. If you store a measure result and then expect that stored value to be recomputed under a different filter, your formula may return an unexpected answer. Keep expressions short enough to inspect and test the changed context separately.</p><pre><code>Margin Status =\nVAR CurrentMargin = [Gross Margin]\nRETURN IF(ISBLANK(CurrentMargin), BLANK(),\n  IF(CurrentMargin &gt;= 0.30, &quot;At or above example target&quot;, &quot;Below example target&quot;))</code></pre><h2 id=\"section-5\">Validate correctness before speed</h2><p>Check the 35% total, the result of selecting one row and the behavior for a zero-revenue test row. Then inspect a representative query and model if a visual is slow. Variables may improve clarity and avoid repeated work in some expressions, but no formula pattern guarantees a fixed speedup. The 30% target in the example is fictional and should be replaced with an agreed business threshold.</p><h2 id=\"references\">Primary references</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/best-practices/dax-variables\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DAX variables guidance</a></li><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-relationships-understand\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Power BI relationships</a></li></ul>",
