    "slug": "power-automate-financial-reporting-workflow",
    "title": "Power Automate for a Financial Close Pack: Approval and Reconciliation",
    "excerpt": "Design a controlled reporting workflow with run identifiers, error scopes and an explicit reviewer.",
    "content": "<aside>Worked example with fictional data. AI-assisted editorial revision; no client performance results are claimed.</aside><h2 id=\"section-1\">Define the output and the approver</h2><p>A fictional finance team prepares a monthly close pack from approved ledger exports. The workflow's job is to collect the right period, check the export, prepare a draft and request review. It does not decide accounting policy or automatically approve a close. Identify the person authorized to release the pack and the conditions that require the process to stop.</p><h2 id=\"section-2\">Validate before sending</h2><p>Record the business period, source file identifier, control total and expected row count. Compare the prepared report with those controls before generating an approval request. A file arriving in a folder is not proof that it is the final approved export. Handle a revised export explicitly, and keep drafts distinguishable from the version a reviewer has approved.</p><h2 id=\"section-3\">Organize error paths</h2><p>Use named scopes or equivalent flow structure to separate validation, main processing and failure handling. Configure the failure path deliberately for failed or timed-out actions. Include a run identifier and a useful diagnostic in the alert, without attaching sensitive ledger rows unnecessarily. Check the connector's current licensing and throttling conditions for the actual environment; a demonstration does not establish production entitlement.</p><pre><code>Receive approved-period export\n-&gt; Validate file identity, row count and control total\n-&gt; Prepare draft pack\n-&gt; Obtain authorized approval\n-&gt; Release approved version once\nFailure path: preserve run ID, stop release and notify the responsible reviewer</code></pre><h2 id=\"section-4\">Prevent duplicate release</h2><p>Create a durable release key such as period plus approved-version identifier. Before sending or publishing the pack, verify that the same version has not already been released. A retry after a timeout must distinguish a provider rejection from an ambiguous outcome where the first send may have succeeded. Do not repeatedly distribute a confidential pack merely because an action did not return its usual confirmation.</p><h2 id=\"section-5\">Exercise exception cases</h2><p>Test a missing file, wrong period, changed schema, rejected approval and a failure after release. Verify that an unapproved draft never reaches the final distribution step. Record how a reviewer replaces an incorrect approved pack and informs recipients. The benefit of the workflow should be measured against actual preparation and review effort; this guide does not claim a fixed reduction in close time or a verified financial return.</p><h2 id=\"references\">Primary references</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/guidance/coding-guidelines/error-handling\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Power Automate error handling</a></li></ul>",
    "sourceHashes": [
      "2aee9a0c24a29d3f91406c6873905fc1186dbeef02783109c5f2627a4e9fa846"
    ],
    "sourceTitles": [
      "Automating the Financial Close: Power Automate Workflows"
    ],
    "reviewedAt": "2026-10-20",
    "readTime": 2,
    "wordCount": 385,
