    "slug": "case-study-n8n-automated-authority-scaling",
    "title": "n8n Editorial Workflow: Collect Sources, Prepare Drafts and Require Review",
    "excerpt": "Design a publishing queue that preserves provenance and prevents accidental duplicate distribution.",
    "content": "<aside>Worked example with fictional data. AI-assisted editorial revision; no client performance results are claimed.</aside><h2 id=\"section-1\">Make the output a draft</h2><p>A fictional editorial workflow collects approved source links, organizes research notes and prepares a draft for a human editor. Its purpose is to reduce coordination work, not to mass-produce authoritative claims or automatically repost other people's articles. Store the source URL, retrieval date and relevant note with each item. A summary is not proof of originality or permission to reproduce the source.</p><h2 id=\"section-2\">Use a stable queue identity</h2><p>Choose an item key that identifies the source and intended article revision. A repeated feed delivery should update or skip that queue item rather than create several publishable copies. Keep draft, reviewed and published states distinct. The editor should approve the actual text, links and images that will be published, not merely the topic name.</p><pre><code>collect approved sources -&gt; deduplicate -&gt; draft with citations\n-&gt; editorial review -&gt; preview -&gt; explicit publish action\npublication does not automatically authorize email or social distribution</code></pre><h2 id=\"section-3\">Handle errors as reviewable work</h2><p>In n8n, use an error workflow or the appropriate node-level error handling for the selected version. Preserve a run identifier and the failed item's key. If a publishing request times out after the remote system may have accepted it, check the recorded publication before retrying. Retrying an ambiguous create operation can produce duplicate posts or notifications.</p><h2 id=\"section-4\">Keep quality independent of throughput</h2><p>Before release, verify that the draft answers its actual question, has working examples and does not invent first-hand experience, tests or customer results. Check rights for any image and the accuracy of every quotation. Track corrections and rejected drafts as well as completed workflow runs. Automation can organize editorial work; it cannot establish that a high article count deserves search visibility or advertising approval.</p><h2 id=\"references\">Primary references</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://docs.n8n.io/flow-logic/error-handling/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">n8n error handling</a></li><li><a href=\"https://support.google.com/adsense/answer/12176698?hl=en\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Google site-readiness guidance</a></li></ul>",
    "sourceHashes": [
      "33f9f6c4e20d4cee2a241fb9e36f3efa6fe097472df7a6e5b271cbde2dd9e924"
    ],
    "sourceTitles": [
      "Case Study: Architecting the 'Auto-Operator' via n8n Orchestration"
    ],
